This research paper focuses on the numerical solution of a stochastic control problem governed by a parabolic equation with the control variable affecting the diffusion coefficient, particularly with the presence of rough inputs; it establishes the convergence of a specific numerical discretization method and provides an alternative solution using a stochastic simulation technique known as the Monte-Carlo method. The underlying stochastic control problem involves the parabolic equation

\begin{cases}
d_u(t) = \kappa(D(t)D_\theta u(t))D_\theta u(t)dt+\sum_{i=1}^N\sigma_i(t)D_\theta u(t)dW^i(t),\quad u(0)=u_0,
\\
\text{ subject to }
u(T)\in\Gamma_T,
\end{cases}

where $0\leq\tau\leq T$, and the objective is to minimize the expectation of the solution $u(\tau)$ over all possible paths. The numerical solution is obtained by discretizing the control variable $u$ using the finite difference method and solving the resulting stochastic finite difference equation, which is validated through comparison with the Monte-Carlo method. Moreover, the paper includes theoretical proofs of the convergence of the numerical solution and provides specific error bounds. Additionally, it offers two applications of the method to solve stochastic control problems involving the Laplace equation and a nonlinear reaction-diffusion equation. The paper also discusses the simulation results for a problem with rough inputs, and it is concluded that the proposed numerical method is efficient for solving a wide range of stochastic control problems. The proposed approach can be extended to solve stochastic control problems governed by time-dependent partial differential equations. The paper comprises six sections, and it concludes with a brief discussion of the results and the scope for further research, which includes the development of more accurate numerical methods and the application of the results to real-world problems. The numerical solution is available online, and it can be downloaded from the website \url{http://www.math.ku.edu/~gao/research.html}. The paper ends with an appendix that provides additional information on the convergence of the numerical solution and the Monte-Carlo method, and it includes a bibliography of 25 references. The research paper is organized as follows: the numerical method is presented in sections \ref{nummeth} and \ref{errorbounds}, and it includes the derivation of the finite difference scheme in section \ref{nummeth}, the convergence results in section \ref{convresults}, and the error estimates in section \ref{
